Hi Iara,

Bolker deals with this problem in the supplemental example <http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0169534709000196> that accompanies his GLMM TREE paper <http://www.cell.com/trends/ecology-evolution/abstract/S0169-5347(09)00019-6>. He suggests several graphical means and provides code to assess which distribution might best help you deal with overdispersion (e.g., Poisson, Negative Binomial (Gamma Poisson), etc.) and in his example ends up using a Poisson distribution with an observation-level random effect. He uses glmer, not mle2, but the use of the observation-level random effect (i.e., create a variable with a unique number for each of your observations and include this as a random effect), can be done with either.

Hope this helps,
Sacha

