A couple of comments: from Evan's description these two model results were different:

> aov(behavior ~ Fish+ Fish:Toads)

> treatment = as.factor(paste(Fish,Toads))
> aov(behavior ~ treatment)

and they should differ, because the first anova assumes that toad presence does not have an effect when fish scent is absent (i.e. when Fish=0, then the effect of Toad is assumed 0).  The 2nd anova does not make that constraint.

>From Rosemary's description, the following two models should be identical... you can verify by comparing fitted values or residuals, likelihood statistics,...etc:

> anova1 = aov(response~ fish*toads)

> anova2 = aov(response~ treatment)
> TukeyHSD(anova2)

but some of the summarized output and especially the coefficients can differ because the predictors are parameterized differently.  This brings me to ask a follow up question -- which p-values are you looking at that are slightly different for the two models?

