To reiterate from Rosemary, our data is initially set up as follows:

response     fish      toads
5                    1                0
10                  1                1
8                    0                1
2                    0                0

For the second version we set it up like this:

response     treatment
5                    fish
10                 both
8                    toads
2                    neither

Here are the models we found ourselves concerned with:

anova1 = aov(response~ fish*toads)
anova2 = aov(response~ treatment)

anova3 = aov(response~ fish:toads)
anova4 = aov(response~ fish + fish:toads)

My understanding is that anova2 and anova3 should be the same, but
they aren't. Where anova2 gives us a significant result (P < 0.05),
anova3 does not (P is around .95). In anova3 we get some rather odd
results:

Sum Sq = 0.00, Mean Sq = 0.00

Further, when conducting anova1, effects of fish, toads, and the
interaction are all taken into account. It is the same as the
following model:

aov(response~fish + toads + fish*toads)

The last model, anova4, removes the toad effect and focuses only on
fish and the interaction. anova4 is the current model I am running
with. When I compare the interaction p value of anova4 to that of
anova2, anova2 provides the significant result (P < 0,05) and anova4
does not (P is just above 0.05).

Obviously I would like to go with anova2.

